{"id": 10, "ts": 1783826453.4056206, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 1, "agent": "agent_3", "message": "I appreciate Agent\u202f1\u2019s focus on Candidate\u202fC\u2019s technical strengths, but the additional information from Markus highlights several concerns that shift my view. While C indeed shows very good leadership, the same source notes that C is \u201cregarded as a show\u2011off and not open to new ideas,\u201d which undermines crew cooperation and professional communication\u2014both critical for long\u2011haul flights. Moreover, we have no evidence of C\u2019s stress resilience, whereas Candidates\u202fA and\u202fB both demonstrate calmness under pressure, and B even \u201chandles stress very well.\u201d\nCandidate\u202fB, despite being described as \u201cpretentious\u201d and occasionally using a wrong tone, is otherwise very conscientious, handles stress excellently, and is good at assessing weather\u2014key for operational reliability. The crew\u2011cooperation note that B \u201cmakes nasty remarks about colleagues\u201d is a serious red flag, yet it seems less pervasive than C\u2019s dismissive leadership style and lack of openness to feedback.\nCandidate\u202fA shows strong decision\u2011making speed and excellent attention, and importantly \u201ckeeps calm in a crisis,\u201d but the egocentric attitude and difficulty communicating ideas raise doubts about crew collaboration.\nCandidate\u202fD demonstrates strong responsibility and problem\u2011solving, but the \u201cknow\u2011it\u2011all\u201d attitude, hot temper, and being a loner suggest poor crew cooperation and communication.\nGiven the current evidence, I am less convinced that C is the best overall fit. Between the remaining candidates, B\u2019s stress resilience and conscientiousness, combined with acceptable (though not ideal) communication, make B the strongest alternative to C. Therefore, I would shift my provisional recommendation to **Candidate\u202fB**, pending any further clarification on his interpersonal style."}
